  "plates": [
    {
      "file": "chapters/01_views.tex",
      "line": 51,
      "figure": "figures/01_views.tex",
      "caption": "The same realization is evaluated in several views. Compatibility is imposed along the arrows, not inferred from the fact that all displayed fibers exist. The ambient family belongs to the simultaneous solution set of the transition equations.",
      "label": "fig:views"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/02_obstructions.tex",
      "line": 73,
      "figure": "figures/02_extension.tex",
      "caption": "The raw family is repaired only along directions invisible on the prescribed views. The obstruction records whether its defect lies in the image of those admissible directions. Once an extension exists, a different kernel measures the remaining freedom.",
      "label": "fig:extension"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/02a_prescription.tex",
      "line": 76,
      "figure": "figures/19_branches.tex",
      "caption": "Every displayed middle value is internally coherent with the initial seed seven. Only even middle values lift to the last view. The initial problem has many global answers, but choosing an odd middle value destroys extendability. The arrows show actual integer preimages, not a probabilistic search policy.",
      "label": "fig:branches"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/03_towers.tex",
      "line": 85,
      "figure": "figures/03_tower.tex",
      "caption": "Exact transport commutes with every adjacent restriction. A cofinal subsequence retains coherent points, but a raw defect can cancel during coarsening. The retained coarse value alone need not record the omitted fine discrepancy.",
      "label": "fig:tower"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/03a_observation.tex",
      "line": 142,
      "figure": "figures/20_schedule.tex",
      "caption": "Independent finite-observation witnesses need not use a monotone input schedule. Taking the running maximum produces one without changing the realized function. The map at the enlarged level first restricts to the originally sufficient level. This construction gives no efficiency bound on how large those levels are.",
      "label": "fig:schedule"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/03_towers.tex",
      "line": 273,
      "figure": "figures/04_split.tex",
      "caption": "In the split coordinate chart, a candidate separates into retained information and compatibility residual. The exact subspace is the zero residual slice. The chart and its protected coordinate depend on the chosen target sections.",
      "label": "fig:split"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/03b_repair_transport.tex",
      "line": 131,
      "figure": "figures/21_mismatch.tex",
      "caption": "The shear preserves the residual coordinate but mixes it into the chosen exact coordinate. Repair after the shear reaches \\((2,0)\\). Repair before the shear reaches \\((0,0)\\). Their difference is the exact-valued mismatch applied to the original residual. The example uses the ordinary Euclidean drawing, while the algebraic identity does not depend on that drawing.",
      "label": "fig:mismatch"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/03_towers.tex",
      "line": 382,
      "figure": "figures/05_cascade.tex",
      "caption": "A split cascade retains every discarded component as a detail. The finite sum is always exact after the restored coarse remainder is included. An infinite detail expansion requires that remainder to vanish in the topology being used.",
      "label": "fig:cascade"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/03_towers.tex",
      "line": 467,
      "figure": "figures/16_dyadic_support.tex",
      "caption": "The possible support of the restored dyadic remainder is sparse and periodic. At depth \\(N\\), only positions whose index plus one is divisible by \\(2^N\\) can remain. Open cells are guaranteed recovered. Marked cells are allowed to survive, not asserted nonzero for every residual.",
      "label": "fig:dyadicsupport"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/04_symmetry.tex",
      "line": 176,
      "figure": "figures/06_groupoid.tex",
      "caption": "The orbit set sees two classes in the \\(C_2\\)-to-\\(S_3\\) example. The groupoid also retains the arrows between conjugate transpositions and all automorphisms. Collapsing the arrows loses information that matters in descent.",
      "label": "fig:groupoid"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/04_symmetry.tex",
      "line": 271,
      "figure": "figures/07_cover.tex",
      "caption": "Object coverage, arrow coverage, and composition coverage are distinct. The proper two-view patches on a three-view chain see every arrow but no patch sees the relation \\(0\\to1\\to2\\). The four proper three-view patches on a four-view chain do cover every composable pair.",
      "label": "fig:cover"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/04_symmetry.tex",
      "line": 406,
      "figure": "figures/08_marked.tex",
      "caption": "The chosen boundary identification is part of a realization. A comparison must commute with it. Under an equivalent presentation, the comparison square may commute through an isomorphism rather than a literal equality, and the marked realization groupoid is still transported correctly.",
      "label": "fig:marked"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/04b_phase.tex",
      "line": 42,
      "figure": "figures/30_phase.tex",
      "caption": "The entry in row $x$ and column $y$ is $c(x,y)=x_2y_1$. The two entries singled out in the text, $c(e_1,e_2)=0$ and $c(e_2,e_1)=1$, already obstruct every scalar flattening. Associativity still holds for every triple.",
      "label": "fig:phase"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 93,
      "figure": "figures/09_holonomy.tex",
      "caption": "Tree routes reconstruct from an anchor. The remaining edges close loops and impose fixed-vector equations at that anchor. A nontrivial loop can obstruct an arbitrary seed without preventing every nonzero seed from extending. The fixed space, rather than the identity of the whole loop operator, is the relevant realization condition.",
      "label": "fig:holonomy"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 167,
      "figure": "figures/10_closed_range.tex",
      "caption": "Residual size controls distance to the exact space only when transverse directions have a positive lower bound. The diagonal example sends unit vectors to residuals tending to zero, so no uniform inverse gain exists. Exact projection still exists.",
      "label": "fig:closed"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 221,
      "figure": "figures/15_protected_geometry.tex",
      "caption": "The same compatibility equation permits different repair problems. Orthogonal repair chooses the nearest point on the diagonal. Preserving the first coordinate forces a vertical correction to a different point. The third coordinate is fixed at five throughout.",
      "label": "fig:protectedgeometry"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 310,
      "figure": "figures/17_amplification.tex",
      "caption": "A flat connection can still amplify a fixed residual. Both orientations of every edge are measured, giving residual norm one. Root-preserving repair has error \\(2^n-1\\), which exceeds the route-length estimate once \\(n\\ge2\\). The vertical scale is logarithmic.",
      "label": "fig:amplification"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 351,
      "figure": "figures/11_descent.tex",
      "caption": "Local correction and overlap correction are orthogonal errors when the second problem is restricted to the locally exact subspace. Their composition is the projection onto the joint exact space. The overlap range inside that subspace is the additional stability obligation.",
      "label": "fig:quantdescent"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 612,
      "figure": "figures/12_moving_line.tex",
      "caption": "The exact space can move continuously without remaining equal to the original kernel. Transport along a chosen splitting preserves a coordinate, while orthogonal projection minimizes distance. In the line example these two maps have different formulas even though both land in the same exact space.",
      "label": "fig:movingline"
    },
    {
      "file": "chapters/05_geometry.tex",
      "line": 623,
      "figure": "figures/18_rank_loss.tex",
      "caption": "Two families can have the same small operator-change norm and very different exact-space motion. A moving line retains a uniform residual gain. The rank-changing diagonal family loses an exact direction for every nonzero parameter, so its projector gap remains one.",
      "label": "fig:rankloss"
    },
